    "reply": "The knowledge graph returns a set of gene records, each carrying the name tumor protein p53 [1]. Each record has its own NCBI Gene identifier, which fits a family of TP53 counterparts in different organisms [2]. The records give only an identifier and a gene name, so this answer can list the counterparts but cannot say which species each one belongs to [3].\n\n## Representative ortholog records\n\nNCBIGene:100009292 is a gene named tumor protein p53 [1]. NCBIGene:100049321 is a gene named tumor protein p53 [2]. NCBIGene:100062044 is a gene named tumor protein p53 [3]. NCBIGene:100092821 is a gene named tumor protein p53 [4].\n\n## Further identifiers\n\nNCBIGene:100435218 is also named tumor protein p53 [9]. NCBIGene:100973941 is also named tumor protein p53 [17]. NCBIGene:101133524 is also named tumor protein p53 [20]. NCBIGene:101589830 is also named tumor protein p53 [30].\n\n## Interpretation and limits\n\nEvery returned record uses the same gene name, tumor protein p53, which points to a conserved gene annotated across many genomes [5]. The records do not state an orthologous_to edge explicitly, so their link to the query gene rests on the shared name alone [6]. To confirm which species each gene comes from, look up the organism for each identifier in NCBI Gene, for example NCBIGene:101558045 [29].",
